# bench/labels/terragoat-aws.truth - the HAND-AUTHORED ground truth for the
# TerraGoat AWS slice, pinned at bench/corpus.lock's `terragoat` commit.
#
# THIS FILE IS THE CORPUS.  TerraGoat ships no machine-readable labels: its own
# ground truth is prose plus Checkov's `CKV_*` check ids, and scoring recall
# against those would bias the result toward Checkov (bench/corpus.lock's own
# note says so, and it is why this corpus read `ground-truth: none` until this
# leg).  So the labels below were authored by reading the Terraform, and they
# are committed - with a rationale beside every single case - because a
# benchmark whose ground truth nobody can inspect is not a benchmark.
#
# NO TOOL OUTPUT OF ANY KIND WAS CONSULTED TO PRODUCE THEM.  That is
# methodology rule R2 ("no using one tool's output as truth for another") and
# it is the one property of this file a reader cannot verify from the file
# itself, so it is stated plainly and the two written rules below are given so
# every call can be re-derived independently.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE UNIT IS THE RESOURCE, and it is the brief's own unit: "which resources
# are genuinely misconfigured".
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# One case per top-level block, covering every `resource`, `data`, `provider`
# and `terraform` block in the slice - none skipped, so a reader can check the
# set is complete rather than curated.  A case's `line` field is the block's
# real extent, brace-matched from the file rather than transcribed.
#
# What the unit CANNOT express, stated because it bounds every number derived
# from this file: a tool that flags a genuinely-misconfigured resource for the
# WRONG REASON still scores a true positive.  Distinguishing "found the missing
# encryption" from "found something else in the same block" would need a defect
# vocabulary shared across scoursh, Checkov, KICS and Trivy, and no such
# vocabulary exists - their rule identifiers do not map onto one another, and
# inventing a mapping here would put an unauditable dial between the corpus and
# the result.  The resource-level unit has no dial at all.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE TWO RULES, applied to every case below
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# R-REAL  A resource is `true` when a competent reviewer would REQUIRE it to be
#         changed before production.
# R-CLEAN A resource is `false` when the configuration as written is a
#         defensible production choice, EVEN IF a best-practice linter would
#         comment on it.  A tool flagging such a resource is counted as a false
#         positive, which is the whole reason the clean cases are here.
#
# R-ATTR  A defect is attributed to the resource whose OWN attributes express
#         it.  A resource that is insecure only because of what it references
#         is `false`, and the referenced resource carries the label - otherwise
#         one underlying mistake is credited to a tool two or three times.
#
# Applying R-CLEAN honestly costs this project findings: it makes an
# unrestricted-egress rule and a public subnet into false positives for every
# tool that flags them, scoursh included.  Cases where the call is genuinely
# arguable are marked BORDERLINE in their own rationale and are re-scored both
# ways in the leg README's sensitivity table, so a reader who disagrees with a
# call can see exactly what it is worth.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FORMAT: bench/lib/truth.sh records - five 0x1f-separated fields plus a sixth.
#
#     <case><US><file><US><category><US><cwe><US><real><US><line-range>
#
# The sixth field is the resource's line range and is what `bench/score.sh
# --match line` scores against: a tool flags this case when it reports a
# finding inside that range in that file.  `cwe` is EMPTY for every case here,
# deliberately - see "what the unit cannot express" above - and the scorer
# renders the strict-CWE column for such a category as an explicit
# `no CWE in truth` cell rather than as a row of zeros.
#
# 71 cases: 33 genuinely misconfigured, 38 correctly configured.

# ==========================================================================
# consts.tf
# ==========================================================================

# data.aws_caller_identity.current  (lines 2-2)
#   A data source returning the account id of the caller. It creates nothing
#   and has no attribute that could be set wrongly. Present so the case set
#   covers every top-level block in the slice with none omitted.
consts:data.aws_caller_identity.currentconsts.tfterraform-awsfalse2-2

# ==========================================================================
# db-app.tf
# ==========================================================================

# aws_db_instance.default  (lines 1-42)
#   publicly_accessible = true on a MySQL instance holding application data,
#   with storage_encrypted = false and backup_retention_period = 0. Three
#   independent production blockers in one block; the file's own seed
#   comments say so.
db-app:aws_db_instance.defaultdb-app.tfterraform-awstrue1-42

# aws_db_option_group.default  (lines 44-63)
#   An option group declaring no options. It carries no security posture:
#   there is no attribute here that could be set wrongly.
db-app:aws_db_option_group.defaultdb-app.tfterraform-awsfalse44-63

# aws_db_parameter_group.default  (lines 65-95)
#   Two character-set parameters. Nothing here weakens the engine's security
#   posture; a reviewer would pass it.
db-app:aws_db_parameter_group.defaultdb-app.tfterraform-awsfalse65-95

# aws_db_subnet_group.default  (lines 97-115)
#   ATTRIBUTION. The subnets it names are public, which is a fact about
#   aws_subnet.web_subnet / web_subnet2 and is labelled there. The subnet
#   group's own attributes express nothing insecure, and crediting the same
#   defect twice would inflate every tool's recall on one underlying
#   mistake.
db-app:aws_db_subnet_group.defaultdb-app.tfterraform-awsfalse97-115

# aws_security_group.default  (lines 117-134)
#   A security group with no inline ingress or egress block at all - the
#   rules are separate aws_security_group_rule resources and are labelled
#   there.
db-app:aws_security_group.defaultdb-app.tfterraform-awsfalse117-134

# aws_security_group_rule.ingress  (lines 136-143)
#   tcp/3306 from ${aws_vpc.web_vpc.cidr_block} - the database port
#   reachable from the VPC and from nowhere else. This is the correct shape
#   for a database security-group rule and is the negative control for the
#   open-ingress class.
db-app:aws_security_group_rule.ingressdb-app.tfterraform-awsfalse136-143

# aws_security_group_rule.egress  (lines 145-152)
#   BORDERLINE, resolved against flagging. Unrestricted egress to 0.0.0.0/0
#   is what AWS itself creates for a new security group, CIS AWS Foundations
#   v3.0 requires egress restriction only on the DEFAULT security group, and
#   allow-all egress on a custom group is the overwhelmingly common
#   production shape. Under this label set's own rule - `a defensible
#   production choice, even where a best-practice linter would comment on
#   it` - it is clean. See the leg README's sensitivity table: this is one
#   of the cases whose flip moves a published number.
db-app:aws_security_group_rule.egressdb-app.tfterraform-awsfalse145-152

# aws_iam_instance_profile.ec2profile  (lines 156-169)
#   An instance profile naming a role. No posture of its own; the role and
#   its policy are labelled separately.
db-app:aws_iam_instance_profile.ec2profiledb-app.tfterraform-awsfalse156-169

# aws_iam_role.ec2role  (lines 171-204)
#   An assume-role trust policy for ec2.amazonaws.com with no wildcard
#   principal. The excessive permissions live in the attached
#   aws_iam_role_policy, which is labelled real.
db-app:aws_iam_role.ec2roledb-app.tfterraform-awsfalse171-204

# aws_iam_role_policy.ec2policy  (lines 206-226)
#   Allow s3:*, ec2:* and rds:* on Resource "*". Full control of three
#   services on every resource in the account, attached to an instance
#   profile a web host assumes. No reviewer passes this.
db-app:aws_iam_role_policy.ec2policydb-app.tfterraform-awstrue206-226

# data.aws_ami.amazon-linux-2  (lines 228-241)
#   A data source selecting the most recent Amazon-owned AMI. It creates
#   nothing.
db-app:data.aws_ami.amazon-linux-2db-app.tfterraform-awsfalse228-241

# aws_instance.db_app  (lines 243-413)
#   user_data writes the database username and password into
#   /var/www/inc/dbinfo.inc in cleartext. EC2 user data is readable from the
#   instance metadata service by anything that can reach 169.254.169.254,
#   and this instance sets no metadata_options, so IMDSv1 is available to
#   any SSRF on the web tier.
db-app:aws_instance.db_appdb-app.tfterraform-awstrue243-413

